{
  "requirement": "`matches` holds the results found. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope. Reaching the exact limit is compatible with a complete result if no further match exists.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SearchResults<T> {\n    pub matches: Vec<T>,\n    pub complete: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<T> SearchResults<T> {\n    pub fn new(matches: Vec<T>, complete: bool) -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations from `requirements.sdk.graph.search.SearchResults`**\n1. `matches` holds the results found.\n2. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope. It is false when a match was omitted.\n3. Reaching the exact limit still gives `complete == true` if no further match exists.\n4. The public shape is `SearchResults<T>` with public `matches` and `complete` fields and `SearchResults::new(matches, complete)`.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- Constructor and fields: `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/search.rs#fn[callers_can_construct_complete_and_partial_search_results]` runs from outside the crate and builds results with `SearchResults::new`.\n  - It builds one result with `complete = false`, using a `TextMatch`. It asserts `complete` is false and checks that `matches` holds the element and its fields.\n  - It builds one with `complete = true`, using a `StructuralMatch`. It asserts `complete` is true and checks the length and contents of `matches`.\n  - Both values of the flag are therefore checked, along with the generic `T` over two match types.\n- Completeness semantics: `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate]` runs `search_text` over a two-hit scope with these limits:\n  - `None` with 2 matches gives complete.\n  - `Some(2)` with 2 matches gives complete. This is the exact-limit boundary the text names.\n  - `Some(1)` with 1 match gives incomplete.\n  - `Some(0)` with 0 matches gives incomplete.\n  - `Some(0)` with no hits in the scope gives complete with empty `matches`.\n  - It asserts both `matches.len()` and `complete` in each case.\n- Other tests I read that assert `complete`: `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` (structural search over limits), `successful_empty_listing_and_empty_resources_are_distinct_from_errors` and `list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search`. These are not marked `{test}` for this requirement, but they add no required coverage.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the type is exported at the specified path.\n\n**Residual point, not counted as a gap**\n- No test asserts `#[non_exhaustive]`, for example with a compile-fail check that a struct literal is rejected. The reviewed tests build values only through `new`. The type's `non_exhaustive` declaration is a declaration-only attribute with no behavior to run, and the prose obligations are what the tests have to cover. They are covered.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
